# Exam track — Describing a Picture: Living Room — Sub-lesson 2: Person Description
#
# The WIEN task for the living-room photo of sub-lesson 1: who is present, and
# one of them described in visual detail. The examiner's second prompt on the
# picture task is almost always "pick one person and describe them" — hair,
# clothes, what they are holding — so this drills exactly that.
#
# The photo detail this describes: four people, two younger and two older. On the
# couch a girl with long dark hair in a dark blue jumper, a woman in a beige
# knitted jumper holding a white cup, and a man in a dark-green jumper with short
# hair and a dark-grey beard. In the armchair on the right a teenager in a grey
# hooded jumper and black trousers. Everyone is wearing jeans or dark trousers;
# the woman and the girl are smiling, the man is talking.
#
# PURE DESCRIPTION, same as 01 — no @question prompts, no first-person opinion.
# A depicted person's visible emotion (`D'Fra lächelt`) is observation and is
# wanted; what the *learner* feels about them is not.
#
# Same A1–A2 constraint as 01, with the guard appearance descriptions make easy
# to break: attributive adjectives are used ONLY on feminine nouns, where
# Luxembourgish leaves them uninflected (`eng wäiss Taass`). Masculine and neuter
# attributives (`e grénge Pullover`, `de jonke Mann`) need declension the learner
# has not met, so those are stated predicatively (`Säi Pullover ass gréng.`) or
# moved into a @fill frame, which the learner reads instead of assembling.
#
# Words sub-lesson 1 already teaches (d'Famill, d'Leit, sëtzen, schwätzen,
# d'Couche, d'Fotell, …) are deliberately NOT repeated — the theme's pass-gate
# makes 01 a prerequisite for 02 and stat keys are shared app-wide. People,
# clothes and colours live here, because appearance is this file's task.
#
# `zwee` and `zwou` are both taught on purpose: Luxembourgish uses a separate
# feminine form of "two", and the sentence about two men and two women is where
# the exam actually needs it.
#
# The three @fill blocks carry the frames every person description reuses: the
# relative clause `e Mann, deen … sëtzt` (verb-final, so it is read rather than
# assembled), the `huet … Hoer` appearance frame with its attributive before a
# plural, and the `hunn … un` clothing frame with its separable prefix at the end.

@lesson L1.02 "Living Room: Person Description"

# Same photo as 01, declared again: @image is per-file, and the learner must see
# it during THIS sub-lesson too. The alt text differs on purpose — it describes
# the detail this file drills.
@image "/assets/exam/picture/living-room/img/living-room.webp"
@image-alt "The same living-room photo, seen for the people in it: a girl with long dark hair in a dark blue jumper, a woman in a beige knitted jumper holding a white cup, a man in a dark-green jumper with a dark-grey beard, and a teenager in a grey hooded jumper in the armchair."

# --- Who is in the picture ---
@word de Mann = the man
@word d'Männer = the men
@word d'Fra = the woman
@word d'Fraen = the women
@word d'Meedchen = the girl
@word de Teenager = the teenager
@word d'Elteren = the parents
@word zwee = two
@word zwou = two (feminine)
@word dräi = three

# --- Appearance ---
@word d'Hoer = the hair
@word de Baart = the beard
@word laang = long
@word kuerz = short
@word donkel = dark
@word donkelgro = dark grey
@word jonk = young
@word al = old

# --- Clothes and colours ---
@word de Pullover = the jumper
@word d'Jeans = the jeans
@word unhunn = to wear
@word gréng = green
@word wäiss = white

# --- Doing things ---
@word d'Taass = the cup
@word d'Hand = the hand
@word halen = to hold
@word lächelen = to smile
@word laachen = to laugh
@word nieft = next to
@word zesummen = together

@sentence
@lu Ech gesinn zwee Männer an zwou Fraen.
@en I see two men and two women.
@distractor-lu dräi
@distractor-lu Elteren
@distractor-en three
@distractor-en parents

@sentence
@lu Zwee Leit si jonk an zwee Leit sinn al.
@en Two people are young and two people are old.
@distractor-lu Fraen
@distractor-lu Männer
@distractor-en women
@distractor-en men

@sentence
@lu Dräi Leit sëtzen op der Couche.
@en Three people are sitting on the couch.
@distractor-lu Fotell
@distractor-lu stinn
@distractor-en armchair
@distractor-en standing

@sentence
@lu De Teenager sëtzt op der Fotell.
@en The teenager is sitting in the armchair.
@distractor-lu Meedchen
@distractor-lu Couche
@distractor-en girl
@distractor-en couch

@sentence
@lu D'Fra sëtzt nieft dem Meedchen.
@en The woman is sitting next to the girl.
@distractor-lu Mann
@distractor-lu Elteren
@distractor-en man
@distractor-en parents

@sentence
@lu De Mann huet e Baart a seng Hoer si kuerz.
@en The man has a beard and his hair is short.
@distractor-lu laang
@distractor-lu Hand
@distractor-en long
@distractor-en hand

@sentence
@lu Säi Baart ass donkelgro.
@en His beard is dark grey.
@distractor-lu gréng
@distractor-lu wäiss
@distractor-en green
@distractor-en white

@sentence
@lu Säi Pullover ass gréng an hie schwätzt mat der Famill.
@en His jumper is green and he is talking with the family.
@distractor-lu donkelgro
@distractor-lu lächelt
@distractor-en grey
@distractor-en smiles

@sentence
@lu Hir Hoer si laang an donkel.
@en The girl's hair is long and dark.
@distractor-lu kuerz
@distractor-lu hell
@distractor-en short
@distractor-en light

@sentence
@lu Si hält eng wäiss Taass an der Hand.
@en She is holding a white cup in her hand.
@distractor-lu gréng
@distractor-lu Jeans
@distractor-en green
@distractor-en jeans

@sentence
@lu All Persoun huet eng Jeans un.
@en Every person is wearing jeans.
@distractor-lu Pullover
@distractor-lu Taass
@distractor-en jumper
@distractor-en cup

@sentence
@lu D'Fra lächelt an de Mann laacht.
@en The woman is smiling and the man is laughing.
@distractor-lu Meedchen
@distractor-lu Teenager
@distractor-en girl
@distractor-en teenager

@sentence
@lu D'Leit sëtzen zesummen a schwätzen.
@en The people are sitting together and talking.
@distractor-lu stinn
@distractor-lu lächelen
@distractor-en standing
@distractor-en smiling

# --- Reusable frames (@fill) ---

# The relative-clause frame — the single most useful shape in a person
# description: it lets the candidate single out one person before describing
# them. `deen … sëtzt` is verb-final, so the learner reads that order instead of
# assembling it, and the comma ends the clause, which is what keeps a later blank
# safe under the Eifeler Regel. Blank is the verb: `sëtzen` is the wrong form
# after `deen` and `steet` is grammatical but false for this photo, so both are
# wrong for different reasons — and a noun cannot fill a verb slot at all.
@fill
@lu Ech gesinn e Mann, deen op der Couche [sëtzt].
@en I see a man who [is sitting] on the couch.
@distractor-lu steet
@distractor-lu sëtzen
@distractor-lu Baart
@distractor-en is standing
@distractor-en to sit
@distractor-en beard

# The appearance frame. `huet [X] Hoer` puts an attributive before a plural noun,
# where Luxembourgish leaves it uninflected — the shape a @sentence cannot safely
# ask the learner to assemble, and one that transfers to describing any person.
# `huet` ends in `-t`, so the blank is safe under the Eifeler Regel (R5), and the
# determiner-free plural keeps the slot adjective-only (R4).
#
# The distractors are impossible hair colours plus a noun, deliberately NOT a
# plausible-but-different colour: `wäiss` would be arguable for an older man, and
# arguable in a blank means a second correct answer.
@fill
@lu Hie huet [donkelgro] Hoer an e Baart.
@en He has [dark grey] hair and a beard.
@distractor-lu blo
@distractor-lu gréng
@distractor-lu Taass
@distractor-en blue
@distractor-en green
@distractor-en cup

# The `hunn … un` clothing frame — the separable `un` sits at the end, another
# order to read rather than build. `e` is fixed in the frame and is masculine, so
# feminine `Jeans`, `Taass` and `Hand` are ruled out on grammar alone (R4) as
# well as on meaning.
@fill
@lu Hie huet e [Pullover] un.
@en He is wearing a [jumper].
@distractor-lu Jeans
@distractor-lu Taass
@distractor-lu Hand
@distractor-en beard
@distractor-en cup
@distractor-en hand
